WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:19.560
<v Ira Glass>Maybe you saw this story about a month ago. This couple was sailing across the Pacific Ocean on a boat with their two young daughters. And one of the kids was three and the other was just a baby, like a year old. And the baby got sick and they called in rescuers. Four Air National Guardsmen parachuted in. A Navy vessel traveled 2,000 miles to reach them.
<v Ira Glass>也许大约一个月前你看到过这篇报道。这对夫妇带着两个年幼的女儿乘船横跨太平洋。其中一个孩子三岁，另一个还是个婴儿，大约一岁。婴儿病了，他们呼叫了救援。四名空中国民警卫队员空降至此。一艘海军舰艇航行了2000英里才到达他们身边。

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:26.600
<v Ira Glass>And this got a lot of play, can I say kind of weirdly, in newspapers, online, on morning TV.
<v Ira Glass>这件事在报纸、网络和晨间电视节目中引起了极大的关注，我甚至可以说，这种关注方式有点奇怪。

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:36.700
<v George Stephanopoulos>Emergency medics parachuting in to save a sick baby on board a sailboat adrift at sea. ABC's Martha Raddatz here with that story. And Martha, so many questions about what that family was doing out there in the first place.
<v George Stephanopoulos>紧急医务人员空降拯救海上漂流帆船上生病的婴儿。ABC的玛莎·拉达茨（Martha Raddatz）来报道这个故事。玛莎，人们有很多疑问，关于这个家庭当初为什么会去那种地方。

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.090
<v Martha Raddatz>So many questions this morning George. This rescue was as harrowing as they come.
<v Martha Raddatz>乔治，今早确实有太多的疑问。这次救援是极其惊心动魄的。

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:50.980
<v Ira Glass>On Good Morning America, George Stephanopoulos and Martha Raddatz updated viewers on the rescue. But also it raised the question that seemed to animate a lot of the interest in the story. That question, were these bad parents?
<v Ira Glass>在《早安美国》节目中，乔治·斯特凡诺普洛斯（George Stephanopoulos）和玛莎·拉达茨向观众更新了救援情况。但这同时也引出了一个似乎激发了大众对该故事浓厚兴趣的问题。那个问题是：他们是糟糕的父母吗？

00:00:50.980 --> 00:01:18.390
<v Martha Raddatz>--but their trip immediately sparking criticism on the family's Facebook page. One person calling them irresponsible parents. Another writing, how selfish to jeopardize the lives of their tiny and helpless children. The family's boat has now sunk, along with everything on it. But this was indeed a very costly rescue. Everybody is very happy the baby was saved. But I don't know about you guys, but when I have children by a swimming pool I'm nervous. Don't know how they did it.
<v Martha Raddatz>——但他们的旅行立刻在家庭的Facebook主页上引发了批评。有人称他们是不负责任的父母。另一个人写道，为了让年幼无助的孩子身陷险境，这太自私了。这家的船现在已经沉没，上面的东西也全都没了。但这次救援确实代价高昂。大家都为婴儿获救感到高兴。但我不知道你们怎么想，反正当我的孩子在游泳池边时，我会很紧张。不知道他们是怎么做到的。

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.790
<v George Stephanopoulos>Yeah. I don't know how they could do it at all. OK Martha, thanks very much.
<v George Stephanopoulos>是的。我不知道他们怎么能做到。好吧玛莎，非常感谢。

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:55.900
<v Ira Glass>The front page New York Times story on the family was headlined, quote, "Two tots, a Sailboat, and a Storm over Parenting," and quoted experienced sailors arguing pro and con weather was OK to take a baby to sea. People went online and they read the blogs that these parents were keeping of their voyage online. And what a surprise, this being the internet they found things to get angry at. Comments on the internet ranged from, why take a baby out to see when she's a baby? After all, she won't remember it. To a point a lot of people made, quote, "This family should be forced to pay for the rescue. Who in the hell takes two little kids on a voyage like this."
<v Ira Glass>《纽约时报》头版关于这个家庭的报道标题是：“两个幼童、一艘帆船和一场育儿风波”，并引用了经验丰富的海员的观点，争论带婴儿出海天气是否合适。人们上网阅读了这些父母在网上记录航行的博客。令人惊讶的是，正如互联网的惯例，他们发现了一些让自己愤怒的地方。网上的评论五花八门，从“为什么要带婴儿出海？毕竟她又记不住”到很多人提出的观点：“这个家庭应该被迫支付救援费用。到底谁会带两个小孩进行这样的航行。”

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:22.110
<v Ira Glass>So that is how the whole thing looked from dry land. But 1,200 miles out in the middle of the ocean in the cabin of a 36 foot boat, it looked pretty different. The parents, their names are Eric and Charlotte Kaufman, say that a whole bunch of things had gone wrong besides the sick baby. The boat was taking on water. Eric is an experienced sailor with a Coast Guard Master's License. It takes 360 days on the water to get that, and it means that he can captain a commercial vessel, which he has done.
<v Ira Glass>这就是从旱地上看整件事的情况。但在距离陆地1200英里的大洋中央，在一艘36英尺长的小船船舱里，情况看起来完全不同。父母的名字叫埃里克（Eric）和夏洛特·考夫曼（Charlotte Kaufman），他们说除了婴儿生病外，还有一连串的事情出了问题。船在进水。埃里克是一位拥有海岸警卫队船长执照的资深海员。获得该执照需要360天的海上航行经验，这意味着他有资格担任商船船长，他也确实担任过。

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:53.700
<v Ira Glass>And he says that when things started to get bad on his sailboat, he started thinking about this piece of gear they have on the boat. It looks like a fluorescent green Big Gulp cup with electronics inside and a little antenna that's sticks out. It's called an EPIRB. EPIRB stands for emergency position indicating radio beacon. And basically when all else fails you break a seal, you push a button, and this device transmits your coordinates to authorities who know that it means that you are in trouble and they need to send help. And on day 16 of the trip-- well here's Eric and then also Charlotte.
<v Ira Glass>他说，当帆船上的情况开始恶化时，他开始考虑船上的这件装备。它看起来像一个带有电子元件和一根伸出天线的荧光绿色大杯子。它叫EPIRB。EPIRB代表紧急位置指示无线电信标。基本上，当所有其他方法都失效时，你撕开密封，按下一个按钮，这个设备就会把你的坐标传给当局，让他们知道这意味着你陷入了麻烦，需要派人救援。在旅行的第16天——嗯，这是埃里克，还有夏洛特。

00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:12.710
<v Eric Kaufman>I just looked over at the bulkhead and there's the EPIRB sitting up there staring back at me. And I ran through it in my head a few times, like have we done everything we reasonably can do? Is this real? Is this really happening? We're not sleep deprived. This is a real thing.
<v Eric Kaufman>我只是看向舱壁，EPIRB就坐在那儿盯着我看。我在脑子里反复想了好几遍，我们是不是已经尽了我们能做的所有合理的努力？这是真的吗？这真的发生了吗？我们不是睡眠不足。这是真实发生的事情。

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:42.960
<v Eric Kaufman>We did the math that we were going to lose the boat. I was  going to have to scuttle it. I mean, we were married and lived on our boat. We were engaged and lived on our boat for that. Both of our kids grew up on that boat. And we were in the middle of crossing an ocean going to the South Pacific. I mean, how idyllic can you get, right? They write songs about that stuff. And instead we were about to push this button that shifts that whole reality into a whole different reality, and a reality we didn't want.
<v Eric Kaufman>我们算了一下，我们要失去这艘船了。我不得不把它凿沉。我的意思是，我们结婚后就住在船上。我们订婚后也住在船上。我们的两个孩子都是在那艘船上长大的。我们当时正在穿越大洋前往南太平洋。我的意思是，还有比这更田园诗般的生活吗，对吧？人们为这些事写歌。而现在，我们即将按下那个按钮，把整个现实推向一个完全不同的现实，一个我们并不想要的现实。

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:52.730
<v Ira Glass>This eventually became national news, the fact that you called for help. Did you have any sense of, OK, so we push this button and now we're famous?
<v Ira Glass>这最终成了全国新闻，关于你们呼叫救援这件事。你们有没有意识到，好吧，如果我们按下这个按钮，我们现在就出名了？

00:03:52.730 --> 00:04:04.600
<v Charlotte Kaufman>No. Never. We were we were completely thrown aback by that. We thought some of the sailing message boards are going to have a thread about this.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>没有。从来没有。我们完全被震惊了。我们以为只会是一些航海留言板上会出现一个关于此事的帖子。

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:24.910
<v Ira Glass>After all, they knew about other boats that had called for help, and it never seemed to land anybody on morning television, or get talked about on The View, or even make the paper. And as for bringing two small children with them, it literally didn't occur to them that that could be an issue for anybody because they knew lots of other families through sailing message boards that they were on with kids just as young as theirs.
<v Ira Glass>毕竟，他们知道其他呼叫过救援的船只，从来没见谁上过晨间电视，或者在《观点》（The View）节目上被讨论，甚至没上过报纸。至于带着两个小孩同行，他们压根儿没觉得这对任何人来说是个问题，因为他们通过航海留言板认识了很多其他带着和他们孩子一样大的幼儿的家庭。

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:38.720
<v Charlotte Kaufman>Oh yes. In fact three other boats did the Pacific crossing this year. The youngest baby was four months old. And in fact, our daughter was the oldest baby. The one-year-old, she was the oldest. The rest were all younger.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>哦，是的。事实上，今年还有三艘船完成了太平洋横渡。最小的婴儿只有四个月大。事实上，我们的女儿是最大的婴儿。那个一岁的，她是最大的。其余的都更小。

00:04:38.720 --> 00:05:03.680
<v Ira Glass>We reached somebody from the American Boat and Yacht Council, which writes the safety guidelines for these kinds of boats, and he confirmed that there are in fact thousands of families living on boats, tooling around the world with children of all ages. And he said that what the Kaufmans did was not unusual at all. In fact, he said, however it may sound to people who do not sail, crossing an ocean on a sailboat is routine. He said it was on the adventurous side of routine, but it was routine.
<v Ira Glass>我们联系了美国船艇和游艇委员会（American Boat and Yacht Council）的人，他们负责制定这类船只的安全指南，他证实实际上有成千上万的家庭居住在船上，带着各年龄段的孩子环游世界。他说考夫曼一家的做法一点也不罕见。事实上，他说，尽管这对不航海的人来说听起来可能很奇怪，但乘坐帆船横跨大洋是常规操作。他说这属于冒险型的常规操作，但确实是常规操作。

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:31.010
<v Ira Glass>So when Eric and Charlotte Kaufman got to safety and realized the reaction their story was getting, they put out a statement on the internet. It said, quote, "For those who are more critical, we ask that you kindly await all the details." They thought that if people heard the whole story, everything that happened to them, their choices would seem reasonable. But they didn't say any more. They didn't say anything else. They gave no interviews explaining what really happened until today.
<v Ira Glass>所以当埃里克和夏洛特·考夫曼到达安全地带，并意识到他们的故事所引发的反应时，他们在网上发表了一份声明。上面写道：“对于那些更具批判性的人，我们恳请你们耐心等待所有细节披露。”他们以为如果人们听到了完整的故事，了解了发生在他们身上的一切，他们的选择看起来就会合情合理。但他们没有再说更多。他们什么也没说。直到今天之前，他们没有接受任何解释到底发生了什么的采访。

00:05:31.010 --> 00:06:02.950
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program we have them and their story. And we also have other stories of people calling for help. It turns out that when you call for help it can lead you to places that you never suspected that it possibly ever could. Including, by the way, we have former Wyoming Senator Alan Simpson stepping in to lend a hand about a matter that is not the kind of matter that one usually turns to the United States Senate for. Not actually the kind of thing that the Senate usually handles at all. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目请到了他们，讲述他们的故事。我们还有其他人呼叫救援的故事。事实证明，当你呼叫救援时，它可能会把你带到你从未怀疑过可能去的地方。顺便提一下，包括前怀俄明州参议员艾伦·辛普森（Alan Simpson）介入帮忙处理了一件事，这可不是那种通常会求助于美国参议院的事情。其实这根本不是参议院通常处理的那种事情。请继续收听。

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:16.670
<v Ira Glass>Act One, When May Day Falls in April. OK. Some basic facts about Eric and Charlotte Kaufman. First off, OK, whatever impressions you have of people who own boats, they are not rich. The boat is their house. They have no other home. This is where their savings went.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，当五月天的求救信号在四月发出。好吧。关于埃里克和夏洛特·考夫曼的一些基本事实。首先，好吧，无论你对船主有什么印象，他们并不富有。这艘船就是他们的房子。他们没有别的家。这就是他们存款的去向。

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:43.460
<v Ira Glass>Before they set sail last month, Eric made his living doing a computer job for a financial services company. Eric and Charlotte had been preparing for this trip seriously since 2010 when they started buying safety gear and did all the careful steps you do to make a ship as safe as possible, replace the rigging and chain plates, that kind of thing. The plan was they were going to travel to the Pacific Islands, make it to New Zealand before November, then live in New Zealand for a couple years, and then on to Micronesia and Indonesia, then who knows where.
<v Ira Glass>在上个月起航之前，埃里克的工作是为一家金融服务公司做计算机工作。埃里克和夏洛特从2010年开始就一直在认真筹备这次旅行，当时他们开始购买安全装备，并采取了所有谨慎的步骤，尽可能让船只变得安全，更换索具和链板，诸如此类。计划是他们要前往太平洋岛屿，在11月之前到达新西兰，然后在新西兰生活几年，接着前往密克罗尼西亚和印度尼西亚，然后谁知道去哪儿。

00:06:43.460 --> 00:07:04.750
<v Ira Glass>When I asked Eric how long the trip was going to be, he didn't have an answer. Years for sure. The idea was they would head out and then just figured it out. Which made me especially interested in the moment that they decided to give all that up and call for help. What happened that made them abandon everything they had been hoping for and planning for for years and years?
<v Ira Glass>当我问埃里克这次旅行要持续多久时，他没有答案。肯定要几年。想法是他们会出发，然后边走边看。这让我对他们决定放弃一切并呼叫救援的那个时刻特别感兴趣。究竟发生了什么，让他们放弃了多年来一直期待和计划的一切？

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:38.370
<v Ira Glass>Well, Eric said that one of the big problems began the day before they called for help. Basically they sailed into a part of the ocean called the Intertropical Convergence Zone where they hit some nasty weather and waves. Not horrible Eric said. You'd expect this kind of thing on an ocean crossing. But they were getting rained on every 30 to 35 minutes with squalls, waves are going over the boat, covering the deck. And then-- here's the bad part-- they get broached, which is sailing language for a wave pushed their boat onto its side, just for a few seconds, and then it righted itself.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，埃里克说最大的问题之一是在他们呼叫救援的前一天开始的。基本上，他们航行到了被称为“热带辐合带”的海域，在那里他们遇到了一些糟糕的天气和波浪。埃里克说并不恐怖。你本就会预料到在大洋横渡中会遇到这种事。但他们每30到35分钟就会经历一次阵雨和飑，浪打过船头，覆盖了甲板。然后——糟糕的部分来了——他们被“横推”了（broached），这是航海术语，指波浪把他们的船推到了侧面，只持续了几秒钟，然后船又恢复了平衡。

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:59.200
<v Charlotte Kaufman>We got knocked over several times. And people have asked Eric, were you scared out there? And Eric always says, no, I wasn't scared. But I was in the cabin with the girls, and one time our oldest was going to the bathroom by herself-- because she's three, she's almost four, and she want's to do everything by herself-- and when that wave hit it's the scariest sound.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>我们被撞翻了好几次。人们问埃里克，你在外面怕吗？埃里克总是说，不，我不怕。但我当时和女孩们在舱内，有一次我们最大的女儿自己去上厕所——因为她三岁了，快四岁了，她想自己做所有事——当那个浪打过来时，那是极其可怕的声音。

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.750
<v Ira Glass>Because it's just a big bang?
<v Ira Glass>因为那只是一声巨响？

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:09.690
<v Charlotte Kaufman>Yeah. It's this huge bang. It's like you were in a car accident. You're expecting to go up and see who just T-boned you. But it was a wave.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>是的。那是一声巨大的巨响。就像你出了车祸一样。你期望往上看，看看是谁撞了你。但那只是一个浪。

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:20.280
<v Ira Glass>And so you guys are downstairs in the cabin and Eric is upstairs driving the boat. Is he upstairs out on the deck when the boat gets turned on its side?
<v Ira Glass>所以你们在下面的舱室里，埃里克在上面驾驶船只。当船被侧翻时，他在上面的甲板上吗？

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:30.900
<v Charlotte Kaufman>Yes. He was definitely on the side decks. But he goes out there clipped in, and we all maintain the rule, one hand for you, one hand for the ship. So he's always holding on.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>是的。他确实在侧甲板上。但他出去的时候是扣上安全扣的，我们都保持一个规则：一只手给自己，一只手给船。所以他总是紧紧抓着。

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:35.060
<v Ira Glass>So he's clipped on with straps and stuff, so he can't just go into the water?
<v Ira Glass>所以他用带子之类的东西扣住了，这样他就不会掉进水里？

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:56.580
<v Charlotte Kaufman>Right. Yeah. I think the hardest part for people who don't sail to really imagine about this trip is that you're constantly in motion. You're burning calories just by sitting because your body is constantly fighting to keep you in an upright position. We could never stand still.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>对。是的。我认为对于不航海的人来说，关于这次旅行最难想象的部分就是你时刻处于运动状态。仅仅是坐着你就在消耗热量，因为你的身体在不断地抗争以保持直立姿势。我们永远无法静止。

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:34.920
<v Charlotte Kaufman>When I'm making food, I have to brace my feet at a really far angle, or I had to wear a belt, a galley belt, just to stay upright. We would have to-- our youngest daughter, she slept in a little chair, a little kid seat, strapped down in her bunk because, if we had just put her in her berth, her body would literally have been just rolling five inches. Rolling five inches. Rolling, because she was too little to lay spread eagle and brace herself at night to sleep.
<v Charlotte Kaufman>当我在做饭时，我必须把脚架在一个非常大的角度，或者我必须系上一条腰带，一条厨房工作腰带，才能保持直立。我们不得不——我们的小女儿，她睡在一个小椅子里，一个童座，绑在她的铺位上，因为如果我们只是把她放在铺位上，她的身体真的会直接滚来滚去五英寸。滚五英寸。滚来滚去，因为她太小了，晚上睡觉时无法张开四肢支撑自己。

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:40.080
<v Ira Glass>Now, to get broached by itself is not such a big deal. Eric says lots of boats get broached.
<v Ira Glass>现在，仅仅是被横推本身并不是什么大不了的事。埃里克说很多船都会被横推。

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:58.320
<v Eric Kaufman>Yeah. I mean, you want to minimize it as much as you possibly can. And you probably did something wrong, or you could have been more active on the helm, or something like that. But it's not like, oh, my gosh, this is horrible. No boat has ever experienced this. That's not the case. It's sailing across an ocean. It's what it is. If you don't want to take hits like that, don't go across an ocean.
<v Eric Kaufman>是的。我的意思是，你会尽可能地减少这种情况。你可能做错了什么，或者你在舵上本可以更主动一点，或者类似的事情。但这不是说，哦，天哪，这太可怕了。没有任何船经历过这种情况。事实并非如此。这就是横跨大洋。就是这样。如果你不想遭受那样的冲击，就别横跨大洋。

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:35.280
<v Ira Glass>The problem, he says, is that when their boat got knocked on to its side, it put the boom into the water. Now I didn't know what a boom was, and so he had to explain that to me. If you picture a triangular sail on a sailboat, there's the mast which runs vertically from the deck, sticks into the sky. And then the horizontal pole that runs along the bottom of the sail, parallel to the water, that's the boom. And when they got broached, the boom went into the water. And when the boat bounced back upright-- the boom is connected to the mast and the rigging, which is connected to the hull with wires and chain plates-- and with all that pressure on the boom--
<v Ira Glass>他说，问题在于当他们的船被撞到侧面时，横杆（boom）进了水。我不知道什么是横杆，所以他不得不向我解释。如果你想象帆船上的三角形帆，有桅杆垂直于甲板，指向天空。然后沿着帆底部平行于水面的水平杆，那就是横杆。当他们被横推时，横杆进了水。当船反弹回直立状态时——横杆连接着桅杆和索具，索具又用钢丝和链板连接到船体——随着横杆上所有这些压力——

