<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。是的。

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:41.750
<v Eric Kaufman>What? You know. Sure enough, they throw smoke grenades out the back and out come-- they kick some gear out the back that comes out on a parachute. So then these four guys all jump out of the back with their parachute gear and they have a Zodiac, an inflatable boat with and outboard on the back. And they press some buttons and [ENGINE NOISE] this thing pops to life and they all hop in it. And then, long story short, they got close to us. We got close to them. And then we bring these guys on board and they just hop on the boat. And then one of the guys immediately went down and started working with Lyra.
<v Eric Kaufman>什么？你知道。果不其然，他们从后面扔下了烟雾弹，然后——他们从后面踢出了一些装备，挂在降落伞上。然后这四个人都带着降落伞装备跳了出来，他们有一个Zodiac，一种带舷外机的充气艇。他们按下一些按钮 [引擎噪音] 这个东西启动了，他们都跳了进去。然后，长话短说，他们靠近了我们。我们靠近了他们。然后我们把这些人带上船，他们直接跳上了我们的船。然后其中一个人立刻走下去，开始照顾莱拉。

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:42.550
<v Ira Glass>This is the baby.
<v Ira Glass>这是那个婴儿。

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:51.200
<v Eric Kaufman>Yeah. They're all very highly trained medical professionals. So one of the guys just immediately went to her.
<v Eric Kaufman>是的。他们都是训练有素的专业医务人员。所以其中一个人立刻走向她。

00:21:51.200 --> 00:22:10.040
<v Ira Glass>The Pacific Ocean is so big that it took three days for a Navy vessel to get to them at full speed. The four guys stayed on the sailboat with the Kaufmans during that time with all their gear in the tiny, non-air conditioned cabin. Six adults and two kids in this small boat. Eric says everybody got to know everybody pretty well.
<v Ira Glass>太平洋太大了，以至于一艘海军舰艇全速赶到他们身边花了三天时间。那四个人在那段时间里和考夫曼一家一起待在帆船上，带着他们所有的装备挤在狭小、没有空调的船舱里。六个成年人和两个孩子在这艘小船里。埃里克说每个人都很好地了解了彼此。

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:26.730
<v Ira Glass>When they transferred off the boat to the USS Vandergrift, Charlotte had the kids say goodbye to their home, and she said goodbye herself. Eric cut some hoses inside the boat which flooded it with seawater and sank his own boat. He didn't watch it go down though.
<v Ira Glass>当他们从那艘船转移到范德格里夫特号（USS Vandergrift）军舰上时，夏洛特让孩子们告别了他们的家，她自己也告别了。埃里克切断了船内的几根软管，导致船体灌满海水，沉没了他们自己的船。不过他没看着它沉下去。

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:38.780
<v Ira Glass>It's been a month now. They never did figure out what exactly Lyra had that made her so sick. And she's still not completely recovered, but she's doing better. And Charlotte and Eric say they do not regret the decision they made.
<v Ira Glass>已经过了一个月了。他们终究没查出莱拉到底得了什么病让她病得那么重。她仍然没有完全恢复，但正在好转。夏洛特和埃里克说他们不后悔自己做出的决定。

00:22:38.780 --> 00:23:08.620
<v Eric Kaufman>And I don't really need anybody to validate that. I know there's people out there who have their thoughts and their commentary about things, sending emails that me and my family should go drown. I couldn't really give a [BLEEP] less what a whole bunch of people think. I mean, we were where we were. We had the situation that we had, and here we are.
<v Eric Kaufman>我真的不需要任何人来证实这一点。我知道外面有人有他们的想法和评论，发送电子邮件说我和我的家人应该去淹死。我根本不在乎一堆人怎么想。我的意思是，我们当时就在我们所处的位置。我们遇到了那样的情况，而我们现在就在这儿。

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:09.600
<v Ira Glass>Alive.
<v Ira Glass>活着。

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:31.410
<v Eric Kaufman>Yeah. So I could flip it around the other way and say I'm a 35-year-old man who made his family homeless. If I'm feeling negative about it, I could look at it that way too.
<v Eric Kaufman>是的。所以我可以反过来说，我是一个让家人无家可归的35岁男人。如果我对这件事感到消极，我也可以那样看。

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:48.120
<v Ira Glass>Eric Kaufman. Coming up, Senators gone wild. Well, one Senator anyway. That's in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>埃里克·考夫曼。接下来，参议员失控了。好吧，至少有一位参议员是这样。一分钟后回来。来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:55.380
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Call for Help. We've arrived at act two of our program. Act Two, Government Assistance.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这个美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。今天的节目，“呼叫救援”。我们进入了节目的第二幕。第二幕，政府援助。

00:23:55.380 --> 00:24:07.370
<v Ira Glass>This next story is about what might be one of the strangest constituent requests of a politician ever. it's told to us by a fellow Public Radio reporter from WNYC, Anna Sale.
<v Ira Glass>接下来的这个故事可能是政治家收到的最奇怪的选民请求之一。它由WNYC的公共广播电台记者安娜·塞勒（Anna Sale）告诉我们。

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:17.180
<v Anna Sale>One day last fall my cellphone went dead at work. I left it to charge on a co-workers desk. When I picked it up a few hours later, there was this odd message.
<v Anna Sale>去年秋天的一天，我的手机在工作中没电了。我把它放在同事的桌子上充电。几个小时后我取回它时，有一条奇怪的留言。

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:31.650
<v Alan Simpson>Ms. Anna Sale. This is Alan K. Simpson in the wilds of Wyoming. Former US Senator. I need to talk to you about an urgent matter. Nothing life threatening at all, I can assure you. But give me a call.
<v Alan Simpson>安娜·塞勒女士。我是怀俄明州荒野中的艾伦·K·辛普森（Alan K. Simpson）。前美国参议员。我需要和你谈谈一件紧急的事情。我可以保证，绝对不是危及生命的事。但请给我回个电话。

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:41.240
<v Anna Sale>I was covering politics at the time, but there was no reason that former Republican Senator Alan Simpson should be calling me out of the blue, urgent or otherwise.
<v Anna Sale>我当时在报道政治，但没有理由让前共和党参议员艾伦·辛普森无缘无故地给我打电话，无论是否紧急。

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:57.510
<v Alan Simpson>Anyway, give me a buzz at your convenience. As I say, I'm in Laramie right now, I was in San Francisco yesterday. Headed for Washington Saturday. And so there your are. OK. Thank you. Bye-bye.
<v Alan Simpson>总之，方便的时候给我打个电话。正如我所说，我现在在拉勒米（Laramie），昨天在旧金山。周六要去华盛顿。所以就是这样。好的。谢谢。再见。

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:19.460
<v Anna Sale>I wanted to call him back immediately, but it was weird enough that I waited a few minutes until I was out of the office. It was the end of the day. I was on my way home walking up Sixth Avenue in Manhattan toward the subway. Senator Simpson, I said, this is Anna Sale. He sounded delighted to hear back from me. And then he started telling me why he called.
<v Anna Sale>我本想立刻给他回电话，但这太奇怪了，我等了几分钟，直到我离开办公室。那是当天结束时。我在回家的路上，沿着曼哈顿的第六大道向地铁走去。参议员辛普森，我说，我是安娜·塞勒。他听起来很高兴我回电了。然后他开始告诉我他为什么打这个电话。

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:38.980
<v Anna Sale>It didn't have anything to do with the national debt or anything else Al Simpson is known for in politics. He wanted to talk to me about my ex-boyfriend Arthur. In other words, Senator Alan Simpson, a total stranger, wanted to talk to me about my love life. I better explain.
<v Anna Sale>这和国债或其他艾伦·辛普森在政界闻名的事情无关。他想和我谈谈我的前男友亚瑟（Arthur）。换句话说，参议员艾伦·辛普森，一个完全陌生的人，想和我谈谈我的私生活。我最好解释一下。

00:25:38.980 --> 00:26:13.500
<v Anna Sale>A month before, Arthur and I had broken up. We'd been mostly long distance for two years. He's an ecologist, and he was spending a lot of time in Wyoming doing field work. He was finally going to move to New York to be with me, but I had the feeling it wasn't for keeps. I asked him if we were going to buy a couch together and he dodged the question. So on the day I was going to sign a lease for our new apartment, we got on the phone and agreed to call it off. After we hung up, I backed out of the apartment and he had to deal with a half-packed U-haul in Wyoming.
<v Anna Sale>一个月前，亚瑟和我分手了。我们有两年大部分时间是异地恋。他是生态学家，在怀俄明州做实地考察花了很多时间。他终于要搬到纽约和我在一起了，但我有一种感觉，他不会长期留下。我问他我们要不要一起买沙发，他回避了这个问题。所以在我准备签新公寓租约的那天，我们通了电话，同意取消。挂断电话后，我退掉了公寓，他不得不处理在怀俄明州装了一半的U-Haul搬家卡车。

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:21.110
<v Arthur>No. It was not half packed. It was entirely packed, dammit.
<v Arthur>不。那不是装了一半。它是完全装满了，该死。

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:37.310
<v Anna Sale>This is Arthur. Our breakup was one of those classic, I love you, but this isn't going to work, breakups. Our version was, we're in our 30s. I'm a reporter in the city. You study wildlife in the wild. This doesn't have a future.
<v Anna Sale>这是亚瑟。我们的分手是那种经典的“我爱你，但这行不通”式的分手。我们的版本是，我们都30多岁了。我是城里的记者。你研究荒野中的野生动物。这没有未来。

00:26:37.310 --> 00:27:02.000
<v Anna Sale>Arthur returned the U-haul. And just a tip if you're ever in this situation, he got all his money back when he mentioned the breakup. A few weeks went by, then Arthur texted and called. He'd changed his mind. He wanted to get back together. Arthur kept insisting he had a plan for how our lives really could fit together. I thought he was panicking about being alone.
<v Anna Sale>亚瑟退了U-Haul卡车。如果你有朝一日遇到这种情况给个建议，他在提到分手时拿回了所有的钱。几周过去了，然后亚瑟发了短信打了电话。他改变主意了。他想复合。亚瑟坚持说他有一个计划，关于我们的生活如何能真正契合。我以为他只是在恐慌独自一人。

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:11.490
<v Arthur>We were just in different places. You thought it was impossible for me to figure all that out, and I knew it was possible.
<v Arthur>我们只是处在不同的地方。你认为我弄清楚所有这些是不可能的，但我知道那是可能的。

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:14.740
<v Anna Sale>And you remember what you said to me?
<v Anna Sale>你还记得你对我说了什么吗？

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:19.860
<v Arthur>Well I basically said, this is not the way it's going to happen.
<v Arthur>嗯，我基本上说了，这不是事情该有的样子。

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:23.990
<v Anna Sale>No. It was even sadder. [CHUCKLES]
<v Anna Sale>不。那更令人难过。 [轻笑]

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:25.310
<v Arthur>Oh really?
<v Arthur>哦真的吗？

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:36.260
<v Anna Sale>You said, Anna, I know you're the only one for me. And I'm one of the only ones for you. I know that.
<v Anna Sale>你说，安娜，我知道你是我的唯一。而我是你的唯一之一。我知道这点。

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:46.670
<v Arthur>Oh, God. [CHUCKLES] Wow.
<v Arthur>哦，天哪。 [轻笑] 哇。

00:27:46.670 --> 00:28:00.890
<v Anna Sale>It was so hard, so sad, but I just wasn't convinced. So I pushed ahead. I got busy with work. I even went on a few dates. I heard from friends that Arthur was a mess.
<v Anna Sale>这太艰难，太令人难过，但我就是没被说服。所以我继续前进。我忙于工作。我甚至约会了几次。我从朋友那里听说亚瑟一团糟。

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:31.100
<v Arthur>And at some point I was just sitting there and I just thought, well, I can't-- there's no argument that I can make. I've said everything I can say. So either she changes her mind or something else has to happen. Something funny has to happen. Things had just gotten too hard, and they had gotten too-- our story had just become too--
<v Arthur>在某个时刻，我只是坐在那儿，我心想，好吧，我不能——我没有任何论据可以提出。我已经说了我能说的一切。所以要么她改变主意，要么必须发生别的事情。必须发生点有趣的事情。事情变得太难了，它们变得太——我们的故事已经变得太——

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:31.750
<v Anna Sale>Heavy.
<v Anna Sale>沉重。

00:28:31.750 --> 00:29:02.020
<v Arthur>Heavy. "Dear Senator Simpson, the love of my life, Ms. Anna Sale, lives in New York City. We've known each other for two years and three months. I love Wyoming and have indulged its pull a little too much. And so a month ago, Anna stopped believing I would ever close the distance to be with her, and she cut me loose. I don't blame her. I was being a fool and I took her for granted, but now I see, eyes wide open, my mistake."
<v Arthur>沉重。“亲爱的辛普森参议员，我一生的挚爱，安娜·塞勒女士，住在纽约市。我们认识两年零三个月了。我热爱怀俄明州，并对其诱惑沉溺得过头了。所以一个月前，安娜不再相信我会缩短距离和她在一起，她把我甩了。我不怪她。我当时是个傻瓜，理所当然地对待她，但现在我睁大了眼睛，看到了我的错误。”

00:29:02.020 --> 00:29:14.540
<v Alan Simpson>"Senator Simpson-- this is the one-- I poured my heart and soul into Wyoming for six years. I hope you will consider this as a favor for a man who's risked it all for that place. Perhaps"--
<v Alan Simpson>“辛普森参议员——重点来了——我为怀俄明州投入了六年的心血。希望你能把这看作是为一个为那个地方冒了一切风险的人帮个忙。也许”——

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:41.880
<v Anna Sale>Arthur wanted Al Simpson to call me and invite me to a ball in Wyoming. All the Wyoming political royalty would be there-- Al Simpson, Dick Cheney. I'd known for months that Arthur was going to be a guest of honor at this ball. He was getting this big prize for Wildlife Research. I was supposed to be Arthur's plus one, until I told him I wasn't going anymore. That's when Arthur wrote to Al. And Al, and his wife Ann, got involved.
<v Anna Sale>亚瑟想让艾伦·辛普森打电话给我，邀请我去怀俄明州参加一个舞会。所有的怀俄明州政治名流都会在那里——艾伦·辛普森，迪克·切尼（Dick Cheney）。我几个月前就知道亚瑟将是那个舞会的贵宾。他要获得野生动物研究方面的大奖。我本应该是亚瑟的舞伴，直到我告诉他我不去了。那就是亚瑟写信给艾伦的时候。艾伦和他的妻子安（Ann）参与进来了。

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:45.010
<v Anna Sale>OK. When you first read that, what did you think?
<v Anna Sale>好的。当你第一次读到那封信时，你想到什么？

00:29:45.010 --> 00:29:59.890
<v Arthur>I just thought it was the auditor, the agents. And I looked at it, put it away, and then Ann and I were in Denver. And I said, I got to do something with this thing. I think I'll just toss it. And Ann looked at it, and she said, worth a try.
<v Arthur>我只是以为那是审计员、代理人之类的。我看了看，把它收起来，然后我和安在丹佛。我说，我得拿这东西做点什么。我想我还是把它扔了吧。安看了看它，她说，值得一试。

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:05.420
<v Ann Simpson>This is just the sweetest letter. And I thought, that would be a shame not to call.
<v Ann Simpson>这是一封极其甜蜜的信。我想，如果不打这个电话就太可惜了。

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:08.460
<v Arthur>"With esteem and humility" he ends.
<v Arthur>“怀着敬意和谦卑”，他结尾道。

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:14.480
<v Ann Simpson>He seemed very desperate.
<v Ann Simpson>他看起来非常绝望。

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:48.420
<v Anna Sale>He was desperate, and some of that had rubbed off on Al. When he reached me on the phone that first time, I got a hard sell. You confessed he didn't know much about us. But he told me all couples have hard times. Then Ann got on the line and asked me, what have you got to lose? It was bizarre. But I was also moved. Arthur's Hail Mary was totally romantic. I told Arthur I'd go to the ball with him.
<v Anna Sale>他是绝望的，而且其中的一些感染了艾伦。他第一次在电话里联系我时，我受到了强烈的推销。你承认他不了解我们太多。但他告诉我所有的夫妇都有困难时期。然后安接过了电话，问我，你有什么可失去的？这很古怪。但我也被感动了。亚瑟的最后一搏（Hail Mary）非常浪漫。我告诉亚瑟我会和他一起去舞会。

00:30:48.420 --> 00:31:16.930
<v Anna Sale>And that weekend in Wyoming was wonderful. The Simpsons were just as sweet and open as they seemed on the phone. They were easy to talk to. Arthur and I ended up telling them stories about how we'd met, and why we'd split up. They chimed in with their own. And then, a few weeks after the ball, Arthur and I got back together. He even moved in with me in New York. And strangely, we kept talking to Al and Ann Simpson.
<v Anna Sale>那个在怀俄明州的周末非常美妙。辛普森夫妇就像在电话里那样甜蜜和开明。他们很容易交谈。亚瑟和我也最终告诉他们我们是如何相遇的，以及为什么分手。他们也分享了他们的故事。然后在舞会几周后，亚瑟和我复合了。他甚至搬来纽约和我同住。奇怪的是，我们继续和艾伦及安·辛普森保持联系。

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:58.630
<v Anna Sale>After that first phone call, we talked a lot. They gave us advice. I needed it. I was still uncertain about the future. Because, look, I'm divorced, I'm in my 30s, I want to be a mother. I was so afraid of making the wrong choice again and losing time. And after things had been so strained with Arthur, it was a relief to listen to the Simpsons who'd been together 60 years but hadn't always felt like they were on solid ground either. They didn't always know what was going to happen together. Ann struggled in their marriage when their three kids were young. And before that, when they were dating Al was drinking a lot, and Ann worried that he was an alcoholic.
<v Anna Sale>在那个电话之后，我们聊了很多。他们给了我们建议。我需要这些建议。我对未来仍然感到不确定。因为，看，我离过婚，30多岁，我想成为一名母亲。我非常害怕再次做出错误的选择并浪费时间。在和亚瑟的关系变得如此紧张之后，听听已经在一起60年但并不总觉得自己在稳固基础上的辛普森夫妇的话，是一种解脱。他们并不总是知道他们在一起会发生什么。当三个孩子还小的时候，安在婚姻中挣扎过。在那之前，他们约会时艾伦喝了很多酒，安担心他是个酒鬼。

00:31:58.630 --> 00:32:25.140
<v Anna Sale>They weren't just telling me these stories to get them off their chest. It did seem like they really wanted to help Arthur and me. It was surprisingly intimate and useful. So much so that I asked if I could come to their house in Cody, Wyoming and talk to them some more. They said, sure. They're like these elder statesman of relationships. They enjoy showing off what they've learned. Take this story about one big fight that started on a dance floor. Al and Ann were at some event.
<v Anna Sale>他们告诉我不只是为了发泄。看起来他们确实想帮助亚瑟和我。这出奇地亲密且有用。以至于我问我是否可以去他们在怀俄明州科迪（Cody）的家中，再和他们多谈谈。他们说，当然。他们就像这些关系的资深政治家。他们喜欢炫耀他们所学到的东西。听听这个故事，关于一场始于舞池的大争吵。艾伦和安在一个活动上。

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:36.570
<v Alan Simpson>And she was dancing with a guy who I knew was a horny old toad. And I thought-- and she loves to dance. And so she's whirling around the floor and giggling and laughing.
<v Alan Simpson>她正在和一个我知道是个好色老癞蛤蟆的家伙跳舞。我想——她喜欢跳舞。所以她在那儿旋转、咯咯笑、大笑。

00:32:36.570 --> 00:33:13.010
<v Alan Simpson>And so anyway, I got home and I said, I want to tell you something. I saw you snuggling up to that horny bastard there. And she said, look, I am not into this to play games. But I'm not going to be under a glass lid just because of your jealousy. And I love to dance. And I will do that. And I'm not going to jump in the sack with somebody, so I think you better get over it. Which really pissed me off.
<v Alan Simpson>总之，我回到家说，我想告诉你一件事。我看见你和那个好色的混蛋在那儿亲热。她说，看，我不是在玩游戏。但我不会仅仅因为你的嫉妒就被关在玻璃盖下。我喜欢跳舞。我会那样做。我不会和谁上床，所以我觉得你最好克服它。这真的激怒了我。

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:33.480
<v Alan Simpson>So I gave her a lecture-- it was a beautiful thing-- about life, and fealty, and love, and loyalty, and all the rest. I thought, she won't sleep a wink. And 10 minutes later, I look in, she [SNORES]. Absolutely dead, but asleep.
